00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.260
So, hi everyone on Facebook and YouTube.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.840
Sal here, for our daily
homeroom livestream,

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.110
for those of you who are
wondering what this is.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.480
This is really just something we started

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.260
when we started seeing school closures

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.110
and social distancing, and we said,

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:03.200
hey, this could be a cool way

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.070
for all of us to stay in touch.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:06.340
And sometimes I have guests,

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.670
and sometimes we just do ask me anythings.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.630
Today, the guest is me, so
we'll just do ask me anythings.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.880
I was just telling the Instagram crowd,

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.570
who is, you know, a little bit lower here.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:18.290
If you ever see me not making eye contact,

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:19.830
they're right over here.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.440
Oh, I guess, is that me on Instagram?

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.020
I don't know, this is bizarre.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.100
Little bit trippy.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.780
But, I was just telling them that I had

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.550
a cracked tooth as of last Friday.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:34.060
I was eating a peach, and which
is not normally considered

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.630
to be a hard tooth shattering thing.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:41.340
But, it did somehow crack
my bottom left molar,

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.590
and so it's been incredibly painful.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:46.900
But considering all that's
happening in the world,

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.720
as I say, it's on the edge of the type

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.430
of problems you wanna have,
'cause obviously there's a lot

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:52.880
of folks going through
a lot more right now.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.520
So, I just got kind of a
emergency dental thing in my,

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.530
this entire half of my face is numb.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:00.850
Let me see, my mom just called,

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.710
so let me, she keeps calling.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:05.210
Maybe one of these days I'll just answer

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:06.710
the phone with my mom.

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:08.790
That could be entertaining.

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:11.990
But yes, this entire half of
my face is right now numb,

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:16.302
so I have an excuse today to sound funny.

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:18.810
(laughs) A better excuse
than I normally do.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:19.870
So, before I start,

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.890
I do wanna make my standard announcement.

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:24.950
Remind everyone that Khan
Academy is a not for profit,

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:26.840
that we exist only
because of philanthropic

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.120
donations from folks like yourself.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.053
So, if you're in a position to do so,

00:02:31.291 --> 00:02:32.700
please think about making a donation.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.010
I wanna give a special shout out

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.970
to several corporations
that have stepped up

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.360
to support Khan Academy
over the last few weeks.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:42.960
Bank of America, Google.org,
AT&amp;T, Novartis, and Fastly.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.490
Thank you for that support.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.050
With that, let's get
in to the heart of it,

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:49.320
and you know, I might cut it short,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:50.960
because all sorts of
weird things are happening

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:51.870
in my mouth right now

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.263
and I apologize if I
have to wipe my mouth.

00:02:55.279 --> 00:02:57.560
Might not be the most pleasant viewing.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:00.318
But yes, half of my
mouth is right now numb.

00:03:00.318 --> 00:03:03.400
I'm a big habit of consistency,

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.960
and habit, so that's why I
want to do this regardless.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:11.060
So please, ask me anything.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.150
All right, some Twitter.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.640
Greg Ferenstein, oh, I know Greg.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:16.840
I have so many questions.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.107
Does your beard have a nickname?

00:03:19.107 --> 00:03:21.320
Will you read me a
bedtime calculus problem?

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:23.110
Can we be friends?

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:25.110
Well, if this is the same Greg
Ferenstein that I do know,

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:26.447
I think we're already friends.

00:03:26.447 --> 00:03:28.747
But maybe this is a
different Greg Ferenstein.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.270
My beard does not have a nickname

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:34.500
that I think would be, you know.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:35.770
I have some eccentricities,

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.450
but they have not quite gotten there yet.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.233
Will you read me a
bedtime calculus problem?

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.373
Maybe, I'll think about that.

00:03:44.373 --> 00:03:46.320
I'll think about how we
can do the logistics.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.430
I'll wait for my tooth to heal

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:50.950
before I think about
bedtime calculus problems.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:52.720
Okay, from Facebook, and you know,

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.200
I said this is a ask me anything.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.640
And it can be literally about anything,

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.350
but I thought last week when
we did the graduation theme,

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.910
that was also fun, 'cause then
I got to be kinda Uncle Sal

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:02.490
and give life advice and wisdom,

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:06.023
and you could take it for
what it, for what it's worth.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.743
So, from Facebook, Lori-Ann Stalshnere.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:12.670
I would love to see
school districts convert

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:13.670
to using Khan Academy regularly.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:16.580
I think the classroom flip,

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:18.050
students watch videos from teachers

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:19.330
or Khan Academy at night

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:20.767
and work through their
assignments in the classroom

00:04:20.767 --> 00:04:23.190
will aid teachers.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.500
Why is this approach not implemented yet?

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.210
So, those of you who don't
know what Lori's referring to

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.610
is something that we've been
sometimes associated with,

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:33.250
but it's not something
that we came up with.

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:35.750
This notion of a flipped
classroom predates Khan Academy.

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:38.760
Arguably this is what,
you know, Socrates did,

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.700
where the students would
do reading ahead of time

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.170
and then when they got to class,

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.880
they would engage in, I guess,

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.260
a Socratic dialogue with Socrates.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.210
But the idea is is that the learning,

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.790
the information retrieval or coverage

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:55.300
happens at your own time and pace,

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.040
and that's especially doable now

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.840
in the time of on-demand video.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.750
And that way, when you get to a classroom,

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:02.920
you're able to dive deeper,

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:04.950
do problem solvings, have conversations,

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:07.150
actually have human-to-human interaction.

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:09.080
And so, that's why people call it a flip

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.850
and once you do that, you
can actually go even further

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.850
because one of the reasons why a classroom

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:15.420
all has to move at the same pace

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.700
is because in a traditional
classroom, you have a lecturer,

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:21.610
and a lecturer by definition
is at a fixed pace.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.130
But all of a sudden, if students
are able to watch content

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:27.230
or even practice at their own
time and pace on their own,

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:29.140
then when they get to class,
they can get more supports,

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.530
and in theory, every student
could go at a different pace.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.870
And you know, I don't
think it has to be strict

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:35.840
videos at home or lectures at home

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:37.990
and then problem solving in the classroom.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.040
I actually think it could be

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.380
whether you're in the
classroom or at home,

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:43.180
you should do whatever
makes sense for you.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:46.070
So, work at your own time and
pace on exercises and videos,

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:47.430
and then when you're in the classroom,

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:49.550
you have extra supports of
your peers, your teacher,

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:52.030
the teacher could look
at the data and see,

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:53.540
hey, so and so's having a problem

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:54.940
or maybe three students
are having trouble.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:56.900
I could have a small
group session with them

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:57.970
while the other students continue

00:05:57.970 --> 00:05:59.080
to work at their own time and pace.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.160
And we've seen this in
many, many classrooms

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.880
and students love it 'cause
it's much more interactive,

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:04.970
they get much more support.

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:07.260
And teachers really like
it 'cause they get to form

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.270
these deeper connections and are really

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.280
able to do differentiated instruction.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.000
To your question of why
we're not seeing more of it,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.910
I think it's just a, it's a different way

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.040
of thinking about classrooms and you know,

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.820
for the most part, it
just sometimes takes time

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:23.310
for people to realize there's a shift.

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:25.630
You know, I've given talks
to groups of educators

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:29.260
and they all intellectually
really agree with this idea.

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:32.480
But it is sometimes harder to move past

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.370
maybe what you're used
to in traditional model.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:37.780
The one, you know, one
silver lining perhaps

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:39.600
of this COVID crisis is that we are seeing

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.270
many, many more teachers
move to this type of a model

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:45.100
because obviously, with
people staying at home now,

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:47.060
you're not getting as
much, I guess you could say

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:48.620
seat time in the classroom.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:52.820
And so, a lot more of
the information delivery

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:55.333
or the lectures or
whatever you wanna call it,

00:06:55.333 --> 00:06:56.920
and the problem solving has to happen

00:06:56.920 --> 00:06:58.370
at the student's own time and pace

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:00.450
and that way when students and teachers

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:02.900
get on Zoom or Google Hangout or whatever,

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:04.180
they have a lot more interaction.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:05.220
'Cause obviously it makes no sense

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:06.950
to just give a lecture on Zoom.

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:09.300
That might as well just be a video.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.910
And honestly, that's not just about Zoom,

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:14.010
that could be true of the real world too.

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:16.270
If you just, you know,
at some universities,

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:18.500
there'll be a lecture with 300 students,

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:20.230
that could and should be a video.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:22.770
And ideally, the students and the teachers

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:24.140
could interact with each other instead,

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.540
which would be far more human.

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:27.370
And if you see me keep shifting my eyes

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:29.400
from a little part of
my screen to another,

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.760
it's because I'm trying
to make eye contact

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:36.070
with both Instagram and the
Facebook, YouTube livestreams

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:38.283
and read the questions, so, my apologies.

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:40.520
All right.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:44.870
There is, let's see, from
Facebook, Ursa Khan says,

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:46.050
I hope you don't mind,

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:48.263
but I tell people that you're my cousin.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.980
Sounds fine to me, for all
we all, we are cousins,

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:53.743
cousin Ursa.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:56.590
All right.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:08:01.590
There are, YouTube, @ForEasin asks,

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:05.350
if you taught at a public
school, what would you teach?

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:08.140
You know, one of the things that I,

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:09.310
when I was in college, I used to say,

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:11.820
if there was such thing
as a PhD in everything,

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:12.840
that's what I would wanna do.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:14.560
'Cause I actually can get very excited

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.130
about teaching almost anything,

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:18.530
or learning about almost anything.

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:21.420
So, you know, math is interesting,

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:22.800
physics, chemistry, biology,

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.100
I would've loved to do literature.

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:26.483
I would love a philosophy course.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.270
I think that's what
keeps things interesting

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:31.760
is where you wanna teach things

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:34.160
where you have some
grounding and familiarity,

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:37.080
but it's also fun to be
on that learning journey

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:38.500
oftentimes with the students.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.540
And oftentimes that makes the teaching

00:08:40.540 --> 00:08:41.580
that much more authentic 'cause

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:44.880
you're that much closer to empathizing

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:45.940
with the experience that the students

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:47.250
are having at that moment.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.040
But you know, in in-person classrooms,

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:52.030
I really like as much
interactivity as possible.

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.670
You know, we started a little lab school,

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.860
it's in our office, it's
called Khan Lab School,

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:59.140
and you know, roughly once
a week or once a month,

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:03.250
I do a seminar with the middle
and upper school students.

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:05.560
And that, we just kinda, you know,

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:06.850
I start with a question.

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:09.320
I just say, what is the
meaning of life, you know?

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:10.759
A small question.

00:09:10.759 --> 00:09:14.350
And then really facilitate
a Socratic dialogue,

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:15.830
so I like doing things like that.

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:19.030
Or I like doing simulations
and games with students.

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:21.600
And I like it when you get in the room,

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.530
the more cross-disciplinary it could be,

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:25.830
so oftentimes in that hour,

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:29.150
we will go into philosophy,
politics, economics,

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:32.889
physics, math, and I think that's

00:09:32.889 --> 00:09:35.910
what really forms the
connections for students.

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:38.850
And frankly, I learn something
interesting every time I do

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:40.080
I think that some of the fun

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:41.280
is when it's not predictable,

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:43.820
when you know, it's not just

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:45.250
from the teacher to the student,

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:46.830
but it's also from the
students to each other

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:48.463
and to the teacher as well.

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:50.463
Let's see.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:53.520
From Facebook, Ganesh Golipeli.

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:55.420
Do you have any plans to
translate content in English

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:56.800
and Indian languages?

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:57.633
Simple answer is yes,

00:09:57.633 --> 00:09:59.747
we have a whole effort going on in India.

00:09:59.747 --> 00:10:02.470
And we have a team in
Delhi and in Bangalore

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:05.700
that is redoing and translating content

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:09.130
into the largest Indian languages.

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:12.270
We also have a project in Hinglish,

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:13.740
for those of you who are not familiar,

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:16.170
that is a hybrid of Hindi and English,

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:19.150
which is apparently a fairly standard

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:21.010
method of learning a lot of topics

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:24.570
or the language of education
in a lot of north India.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:26.590
So yeah, we are doing that, simple answer.

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:29.480
So, look that up, look up,
you know, Khan Academy India,

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.720
and I think you should
be able to find that.

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:35.240
Okay, from Facebook, Kasava Riman asks,

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:37.340
how long did you study?

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:40.410
And how long do you recommend study?

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:42.490
How are you motivated to
study when you were young?

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:44.270
So, I guess that question, you know,

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:46.920
do you mean how many
years did I go to school?

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:50.020
Or when I studied in school,
how long did I study?

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:52.010
I guess I'll answer both.

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:54.560
In terms, I guess the first
one's easier to answer.

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:58.150
You know, my undergrad,
I did a master's degree,

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:00.590
but all of that stuff
happened in about four years.

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:01.937
And that was in CS and math

00:11:01.937 --> 00:11:05.440
and a master's in computer
science, electrical engineering,

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:06.860
and then I went to business
school for two years.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:09.100
So you can add up.

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:11.800
And then I was working
for two and a half years

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:13.940
between undergraduate and business school.

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:15.620
So that's how long I went to school,

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:18.000
formal schooling, I guess you could say.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:19.680
In terms of how long did I study,

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:21.090
it really depended on the context.

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:23.730
And depending on what I
was doing at the time.

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:28.010
What I found in courses
like math and physics

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:31.560
and chemistry and biology,
essentially STEM courses,

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:33.860
or even, you know, programming courses,

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:36.340
I found that if you do the problem sets,

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:39.013
and you're always just thinking about it,

00:11:39.013 --> 00:11:41.480
then you actually don't
need to do a lot of studying

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:42.390
or a lot of cramming.

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:45.600
If, you know, the professor
is about to do a problem,

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:48.040
try to do it ahead of time or
try to do it at the same time

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:49.490
that the professor's doing it.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:50.950
If you're doing all the problem sets,

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:52.110
you're doing all the projects,

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:54.713
I found that the tests are
actually quite intuitive.

00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:56.620
You know, I have peers,

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:58.090
and I don't think it's
anything about intelligence,

00:11:58.090 --> 00:11:59.780
who sometimes would, you know,

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:03.360
they're kind of mindlessly
note-taking in a class,

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:05.280
and maybe not actively learning.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:06.580
And then they find that when they go

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:08.260
right before the final exam,

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:09.860
they have to do a lot of cramming.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:12.750
And so, I'm a much bigger
fan of active learning.

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:14.460
You know, in one old school house,

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:16.993
I was a little bit
infamous in college that,

00:12:16.993 --> 00:12:19.550
you know, certain lectures
I found a lot of value in

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:21.330
and I found that I could
do that active learning

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:23.710
and I could, and then others, I did not.

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:25.440
And so, I sometimes didn't go to them,

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:29.930
and instead, I would go and I
would get get out the textbook

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:32.110
and I would do problems on my own.

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:34.090
And I found if I did that all term,

00:12:34.090 --> 00:12:35.320
then when I got to the class,

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:37.210
then I would, you know,
when I got the exam,

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:39.440
I had to do a lot less real studying.

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:41.020
But other subjects that might've had,

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:42.380
you know, more content to them,

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:45.058
or more terminology, you know,

00:12:45.058 --> 00:12:48.040
I wish I could say that
I wasn't, you know,

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:50.300
a super procrastinator, but
there were times in college

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:52.513
that I pulled at
all-nighters and you know,

00:12:52.513 --> 00:12:56.760
and in hindsight, I wish that
I had done it more regularly,

00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:59.010
you know, the more
mature kind of, you know,

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:01.990
fully developed frontal
lobe Sal would've said,

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:03.140
yeah, you know, if I just worked on it

00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:04.210
a couple hours every day,

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:05.980
then I would've not had to
pull those all-nighters,

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:08.710
but you know, in college,
you might find yourself,

00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:10.550
you know, three, four, five, six hours

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:12.380
the night before a test
if you haven't been

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:13.940
doing what you're
supposed to up until then,

00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:15.740
you might have to do that type of thing.

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:18.430
But I don't condone it.

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:19.530
But yeah, you know, you're going to have

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:21.670
to be the judge yourself
of how much to study.

00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:23.850
But I think the most important
thing in everything in life,

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:25.620
you know, I say this about Khan Academy,

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:27.490
it's okay to binge do
it every now and then,

00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:29.613
but the better is to form a regular habit,

00:13:29.613 --> 00:13:33.260
20, 30, 40 minutes a day in anything

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:35.640
is going to actually build those muscles.

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:37.580
And frankly, not only will you not

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:38.920
have to cram for the test,

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:40.540
but you're likely to
have better retention.

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:43.010
'Cause oftentimes you
just cram for the test,

00:13:43.010 --> 00:13:45.610
you take the test, you
go sleep for 16 hours,

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:47.463
and you wake up, you're
like, what just happened?

00:13:47.463 --> 00:13:51.150
(laughs) You might not remember
a lot of what just happened.

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:56.150
All right, so there's, from
Facebook, oh, this is Duo,

00:13:58.460 --> 00:13:59.980
this is Khan Academy Duo.

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:03.320
Tell us why you love some

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:05.630
of your favorite science
fiction authors, please.

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:07.867
And then Dan from Khan Academy is asking,

00:14:07.867 --> 00:14:10.893
"Dune" or "Foundation," which
one do you prefer and why?

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:15.220
So, to answer Duo, you know, some of my,

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:18.620
well, the second question
from Dan kinda answers.

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:21.270
You know, I'm a big Arthur C. Clarke fan.

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:24.392
Obviously, kind of the
whole "2001" series,

00:14:24.392 --> 00:14:29.392
the whole notion of, I also
like "Childhood's End."

00:14:29.510 --> 00:14:31.632
There's a bunch of good
Arthur C. Clarke stuff

00:14:31.632 --> 00:14:33.580
that are very enjoyable.

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:35.743
Isaac Asimov I've talked a lot about.

00:14:37.419 --> 00:14:40.280
And George Herbert, "Dune," I mean,

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:44.260
you can see behind me a lot
of these books are there.

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:46.840
Let's see, can I, where's the "Dune?"

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:49.575
But you know, I'm not a big believer

00:14:49.575 --> 00:14:52.800
in stack ranking everything in your life.

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:55.067
You know, it's like stack
ranking your children, you know?

00:14:55.067 --> 00:15:00.067
You love them all and they all
add dimensions to your life.

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:03.540
But I'll tell you why I
loved many of these things.

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:06.430
You know, "Foundation," I've
talked about it many times,

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:09.002
the "Foundation" series, I'm
not giving the plot away,

00:15:09.002 --> 00:15:10.423
it's a good read.

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:13.020
And I envy any of y'all
who haven't read it

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:15.340
because you get to enjoy
it for the first time.

00:15:15.340 --> 00:15:17.690
But it takes place 30,000
years in the future,

00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:19.860
humanity has colonized the galaxy.

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:23.790
And there's kind of this
mathematician, historian,

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:27.300
you know, psychologist-type
academic named Hari Seldon

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:31.240
who develops this
science of psychohistory.

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:34.950
And he, through his math, shows that

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:37.720
there's a very high probability
that the galactic empire

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:42.110
is about to enter into
a 10,000 year dark ages.

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:44.050
And he decides to do something about it.

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:46.920
His math shows him, his science shows him

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:49.500
that if he's able to collect the galaxy's,

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:50.870
the civilization's knowledge,

00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:53.950
and put it in the periphery
of the galaxy as a foundation,

00:15:53.950 --> 00:15:55.470
that will shorten the dark ages

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:58.223
from 10,000 years to 1,000 years.

00:15:59.060 --> 00:16:01.800
And you know, when I read that as a kid,

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:04.150
I think I first read it in middle school,

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:05.660
I was like, this is epic.

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:06.493
Well, you know, first of all,

00:16:06.493 --> 00:16:07.840
it was the first time I'd
really appreciated that,

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:08.770
you know what?

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:10.450
Hari Seldon is right.

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:13.770
The single most important
nugget for civilization

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:15.630
or for people is at the end of the day

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:17.760
how much they're able to
leverage their human potential,

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.340
and that all boils down
to, really, knowledge.

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:22.440
And so, that's number one,

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:25.390
and that probably put a
seed in my mind later on

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:27.930
when I was working on
Khan Academy that like,

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:29.220
this could matter a lot.

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:32.180
Maybe Khan Academy could be
this foundation for the world

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:34.410
that could maybe prevent us
from getting into a dark age

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:37.560
or maybe elevate humanity
to a whole other level,

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:38.393
whatever else.

00:16:38.393 --> 00:16:42.280
And the other thing that I
was inspired by by Hari Seldon

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:45.280
was that he thought on that timeframe.

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:47.580
You know, most of us do not think,

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:49.780
oh, what's going to happen
in a thousand years?

00:16:49.780 --> 00:16:51.530
What can I help to keep, you know,

00:16:51.530 --> 00:16:56.260
that 10,000 years of
pain and war and famine

00:16:56.260 --> 00:16:58.100
instead being 1,000
years, 'cause you know,

00:16:58.100 --> 00:16:58.933
a lot of us said, well, you know,

00:16:58.933 --> 00:17:00.300
I've probably got another, I don't know,

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:01.920
60 years on this planet at best.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:03.480
I mean, many of you young people watching

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:05.890
hopefully will have many, many more years.

00:17:05.890 --> 00:17:07.830
And you know, we can talk about, you know,

00:17:07.830 --> 00:17:09.530
this whole notion of the singularity

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:13.460
and if medical science can
extend lifespans, who knows?

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:16.800
There might be some generation
that lives much, much longer.

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:18.280
Actually, I think we're
gonna bring David Sinclair,

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:19.240
who's done research on that,

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:21.510
onto our livestream at some point.

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:23.560
But you know, I was inspired that by,

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:26.270
well, maybe we should be
thinking on type of a timeframe

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:30.330
because that's probably
what's gonna help humanity.

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:31.960
And it doesn't even
have to be 10,000 years

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:32.910
or a thousand years, but you can think

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:34.940
on a hundred-year timeframe
or a 50-year timeframe,

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:36.460
which I found incredibly inspiring.

00:17:36.460 --> 00:17:38.140
And that I've told many of y'all

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:39.660
that was one of the reasons why

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:41.460
I set up Khan Academy as a not for profit.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:42.780
It was illusional 10 years ago

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:45.570
when I was operating in this
very same walk-in closet

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:47.010
that I'm in right now.

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:50.360
And but I said, you know,
maybe Khan Academy could be

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:52.280
like the Foundation, it could serve,

00:17:52.280 --> 00:17:55.433
one day, billions of kids
for hundreds of years.

00:17:56.350 --> 00:17:58.060
And be one of the great
institutions of the world.

00:17:58.060 --> 00:18:01.230
And it was frankly outright
delusional back then,

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:02.990
but it's feeling a lot
less delusional now.

00:18:02.990 --> 00:18:04.700
You know, we have a hundred
million registered users,

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:05.940
there's no reason can't get,

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:08.460
we've grown a hundredfold
over the last 10 years.

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:10.960
Why couldn't we grow another
tenfold and get to a billion?

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:12.830
And really build out the
content and the software

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:14.960
and the tools and all of
the stuff so that we really,

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:18.400
you know, that, no matter
where you are born,

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:20.580
if you at least have access
to a low cost device,

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:22.740
and the devices are
getting cheaper every day,

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:25.540
and internet access,
then you have a lifeline.

00:18:25.540 --> 00:18:29.470
It could be in some village
that, you know, has no school.

00:18:29.470 --> 00:18:30.950
You still have a lifeline.

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:32.520
Or it could be a place
that does have a school

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:34.200
and then you're able to use this thing

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:36.520
to empower the school,
to empower the teachers,

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:38.230
so that you get more personalization

00:18:38.230 --> 00:18:40.280
and more access to more content.

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:42.040
And you know, there's ideas of, you know,

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:43.880
ways to pair people that one student

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:45.170
could tutor another student,

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:46.700
even if they're in
completely different places

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:48.580
or different countries.

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:50.837
So, anyway, that's "Foundation."

00:18:50.837 --> 00:18:53.277
"Dune" is really, you know,

00:18:53.277 --> 00:18:55.240
"Dune" is epic because
it also takes place,

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:57.530
I think, several thousand
years in the future.

00:18:57.530 --> 00:18:58.690
And you know, it's obviously,

00:18:58.690 --> 00:19:03.330
it's kind of an allegory for the whole,

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:05.979
you know, energy, and whether, you know,

00:19:05.979 --> 00:19:08.270
many would argue that, you know,

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:09.750
because of oil in the Middle East,

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:13.870
that has made the Middle
East a very tough,

00:19:13.870 --> 00:19:16.960
you know, especially
coming out of World War I,

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:19.190
when people realized that
oil was very valuable

00:19:19.190 --> 00:19:21.083
and the Ottoman Empire was broken up.

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:24.590
You know, that's why
you had that instability

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:28.420
because there'd been so
much interest in oil,

00:19:28.420 --> 00:19:30.260
which is, you know, obviously

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:32.130
it can be an issue of national security

00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:33.950
and definitely an issue
of economic security.

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:35.240
And so, "Dune" takes place, I think,

00:19:35.240 --> 00:19:37.740
3,000 years in the future,
but it's the same thing,

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:41.290
that there's this planet,
Arrakis, that you can mine spice,

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:43.020
and spice is used by the navigators

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:45.530
to be able to do interstellar travel.

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:47.220
And so it's analogous to oil,

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:48.990
but that makes Arrakis, which has a lot

00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:51.500
of parallels to the Middle East,

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:52.950
it's kind of a desert planet,

00:19:53.990 --> 00:19:55.970
and it has some of these, you know,

00:19:55.970 --> 00:19:58.190
there's some nomadic
people who live there.

00:19:58.190 --> 00:20:00.380
But it makes them kind of a tension point,

00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:01.900
and so there's kind of a power grab there.

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:06.464
So, it's a good metaphor for
geopolitics and politics,

00:20:06.464 --> 00:20:09.949
but then there's also a bit
of a metaphysical layer on it

00:20:09.949 --> 00:20:14.360
where the main character
is kind of perceived

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:18.450
as the kind of the savior
on a religious level,

00:20:18.450 --> 00:20:19.780
you know, there's this concept in Islam

00:20:19.780 --> 00:20:22.850
known as the Mahdi, which
is kind of the savior,

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:26.590
the person who comes, you
know, at some future point.

00:20:26.590 --> 00:20:30.270
And so, the people who live on Arrakis

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:32.360
are kind of this neo-Islamic, you know,

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:34.310
you can imagine how religions might evolve

00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:37.000
over thousands of years, and
so, they still have retained

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:38.810
and they think the main character is that,

00:20:38.810 --> 00:20:41.150
and he does seem to have certain powers

00:20:41.150 --> 00:20:43.670
or data points to back that up.

00:20:43.670 --> 00:20:44.960
So anyway, as you can imagine,

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:48.260
anything that is truly
epic and expands your mind,

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:50.570
and makes you kind of think beyond the,

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:53.200
oh, I have to go to a dentist
appointment to remove my tooth

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:55.900
or you know, I better go buy
some baking soda tonight,

00:20:55.900 --> 00:20:57.300
and instead makes you think about, like,

00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:59.890
wow, what are we?

00:20:59.890 --> 00:21:02.880
And where are in the whole
scope of time and space?

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:06.090
And what could we become
as a civilization?

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:09.020
I think is always really interesting.

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:11.660
But you know, I think we sometimes forget

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:15.980
that we are an intermediary
on some form of pathway,

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:19.220
of some form of, you know,

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:21.210
humanity a thousand years from now

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:24.470
might be very, very different
than humanity today.

00:21:24.470 --> 00:21:26.490
But we, you know, us, the collective we,

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:27.810
are the people who are going to kind of

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:29.550
define what that trajectory is,

00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:32.950
so it's just really interesting
things to think about.

00:21:32.950 --> 00:21:35.029
So, let's see.

00:21:35.029 --> 00:21:37.740
Virvinder Bojwani asks,

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:40.080
what are the top
